{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1512998-how-do-you-handle-disputes","id":1512998,"title":"How do you handle disputes","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-05-27T13:11:37-07:00","id":1512998,"title":"How do you handle disputes","content":"So, I bring another interesting topic (well, at least I think it's interresting lol) for discussion, mainly aimed at Storytellers. So, how do you handle disputes/battles/conflicts or whatever that only involves NPCs in your games? <br /> I mean, surely we as Storytellers can simply decide who wins a dispute, but for some reason I don't think it's particularly fun and sometimes when I have a chance I create a dispute (when I was younger, I used to even make all the rolls and describe the scenes even if it happened outside the PCs view, nerdy I know lol) involving dice or something else, but then it made me curious at how other Storytellers handle such things. Do you always predetermine the results of conflicts that only involve NPCs or do you sometimes make a &quot;mini-game&quot;, roll dice or any other method to decide the result?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-27T15:13:01-07:00","id":1513006,"title":"","content":"I don't bother rolling out such things, but I also try my best to only include them if the PCs are &quot;involved&quot; even if indirectly. Who wins the Prince's favor in a debate between Primogen, in my games, depends on what the PCs have done that pushes the status of the debate towards one side or another. If the players can't see how their PC's actions are impacting something, I don't have a reason to &quot;show&quot; it to them in the first place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-27T18:07:10-07:00","id":1513019,"title":"","content":"I have noticed that the introduction of a random element adds a lot of depth and unexpected turns to the game.<br /> I use this web<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://jamesturneronline.net/game-masters-apprentice/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://jamesturneronline.net/game-masters-apprentice/\" target=\"_blank\"> GM's Apprentice </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://jamesturneronline.net/game-masters-apprentice/\" target=\"_blank\"> https://jamesturneronline.net/game-masters-apprentice/ </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Instant inspiration for tabletop RPG game masters and solo role-players </div> </div> </div><br /> generally assigning odds based on the NPC's abilities and the nature of their conflict.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27429-microcuchon","name":"Microcuchon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"201"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-27T21:28:45-07:00","id":1513037,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1512998-how-do-you-handle-disputes?p=1513006#post1513006\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't bother rolling out such things, but I also try my best to only include them if the PCs are &quot;involved&quot; even if indirectly. Who wins the Prince's favor in a debate between Primogen, in my games, depends on what the PCs have done that pushes the status of the debate towards one side or another. If the players can't see how their PC's actions are impacting something, I don't have a reason to &quot;show&quot; it to them in the first place.</div> </div> </div> </div>I also try my best to include the PCs in such scenarios, as it's expected, but sometimes they just aren't really involved, and I feel even a bit uncomfortable sometimes, to decide alone who would win in such scenarios, that's why I like to rely on some system to determine who wins in these conflicts. But, for instance, perhaps to better illustrate, there are instances when the players would prefer a specific NPC to win the conflict, but to decide that only on their preferences seems a bit silly to me, because some NPCs have obvious advantages in conflicts with others. That's why I asked you how you prefer to handle such conclicts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I have noticed that the introduction of a random element adds a lot of depth and unexpected turns to the game.<br /> I use this web\u200b </div> </div> </div>Yes! Me too, I love that adding other random elements can add a lot of depth to the game, I'm just not sure if I understood correctly the link you presented. Would you care to explain it better?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-27T21:31:01-07:00","id":1513038,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't bother rolling out such things, but I also try my best to only include them if the PCs are &quot;involved&quot; even if indirectly. Who wins the Prince's favor in a debate between Primogen, in my games, depends on what the PCs have done that pushes the status of the debate towards one side or another. If the players can't see how their PC's actions are impacting something, I don't have a reason to &quot;show&quot; it to them in the first place.\u200b </div> </div> </div>I also try my best to include the PCs in such scenarios, as it's expected, but sometimes they just aren't really involved, and I feel even a bit uncomfortable sometimes, to decide alone who would win in such scenarios, that's why I like to rely on some system to determine who wins in these conflicts. But, for instance, perhaps to better illustrate, there are instances when the players would prefer a specific NPC to win the conflict, but to decide that only on their preferences seems a bit silly to me, because some NPCs have obvious advantages in conflicts with others. That's why I asked you how you prefer to handle such conflicts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I have noticed that the introduction of a random element adds a lot of depth and unexpected turns to the game.<br /> I use this web\u200b </div> </div> </div>Yes! Me too, I love that adding other random elements can add a lot of depth to the game and it doesn't feel like &quot;I'm writing a romance&quot; or anything, I'm just not sure if I understood correctly the link you presented. Would you care to explain it better?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-27T22:03:28-07:00","id":1513039,"title":"","content":"In general, my principle is that I avoid predetermining things in an RPG, I see it as a game and as such different from writing a romance. Even though, sometimes we want a certain NPC or even PC, in some cases, destroyed to make the story progress as we see fit, I think it's better when we give &quot;fate&quot; its due, so that there's always at least a chance(howerver small it might be) that a certain NPC or PC is going to survive and/or win, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-27T22:13:17-07:00","id":1513040,"title":"","content":"If the players are present and have the chance to get involved then I'll probably do rolls. In those situations I'm expecting they might interfere. (Note this is assuming a potential even odds scenario if its say an execution the other side is just struggling against their guards and bonds then yeah it goes along the rails.) If the players aren't involved then I usually have an idea how I want events to play out and, so absent player interference or players having done something that would upset my idea of how the event would happen, an event offscreen is already decided and I might roll to decide certain scales or factors that are more up for grabs like how in favor of a specific side a deal goes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-27T22:49:58-07:00","id":1513043,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nalak42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1512998-how-do-you-handle-disputes?p=1513040#post1513040\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the players are present and have the chance to get involved then I'll probably do rolls. In those situations I'm expecting they might interfere. (Note this is assuming a potential even odds scenario if its say an execution the other side is just struggling against their guards and bonds then yeah it goes along the rails.) If the players aren't involved then I usually have an idea how I want events to play out and, so absent player interference or players having done something that would upset my idea of how the event would happen, an event offscreen is already decided and I might roll to decide certain scales or factors that are more up for grabs like how in favor of a specific side a deal goes.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see, in general I mean events that somehow affect the PCs. Most of the time I have a very in depth idea of how things are interconnected in my game, though different layers (and so on), so that &quot;NPC x&quot; winning a given conflict might result in a very different scenario from &quot;NPC y&quot; winning, etc. I'm thinking about that specifically because probably in the next chapter (game session, if everything goes according to plan), two important NPCs are going to confront each other over a certain issue and basically everything in the story is going to depend on the result of this conflict, so if one NPC gets to become &quot;the next monarch&quot; it's going to be a very different scenario if another NPC gets what he wants. For me, it's obvious which NPC the PCs would want to win in the struggle, so that made me curious about how other Storytellers would handle such a situation as at this point the PCs are &quot;outside the playing field&quot;, but are still going to be affected anyway by the results of the conflict. So, in this scenario, I feel it's unfair to just decide by myself which NPC is going to win, on one hand making the NPC they want to win get what he wants is surely going to make their (un)lives a lot easier, but on the other hand, if the other NPC wins it's going to be the opposite, I feel that deciding it without resorting to any &quot;random element&quot;, like dice, etc, &quot;luck&quot; or &quot;fate&quot; in itself, is in the end unfair in a game where &quot;luck&quot; has an important role.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-28T00:17:49-07:00","id":1513047,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1512998-how-do-you-handle-disputes?p=1513043#post1513043\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I see, in general I mean events that somehow affect the PCs. Most of the time I have a very in depth idea of how things are interconnected in my game, though different layers (and so on), so that &quot;NPC x&quot; winning a given conflict might result in a very different scenario from &quot;NPC y&quot; winning, etc. I'm thinking about that specifically because probably in the next chapter (game session, if everything goes according to plan), two important NPCs are going to confront each other over a certain issue and basically everything in the story is going to depend on the result of this conflict, so if one NPC gets to become &quot;the next monarch&quot; it's going to be a very different scenario if another NPC gets what he wants. For me, it's obvious which NPC the PCs would want to win in the struggle, so that made me curious about how other Storytellers would handle such a situation as at this point the PCs are &quot;outside the playing field&quot;, but are still going to be affected anyway by the results of the conflict. So, in this scenario, I feel it's unfair to just decide by myself which NPC is going to win, on one hand making the NPC they want to win get what he wants is surely going to make their (un)lives a lot easier, but on the other hand, if the other NPC wins it's going to be the opposite, I feel that deciding it without resorting to any &quot;random element&quot;, like dice, etc, &quot;luck&quot; or &quot;fate&quot; in itself, is in the end unfair in a game where &quot;luck&quot; has an important role.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well part of it would also come down to why the players would prefer one side over the other I'd think. If the party prefers claimant A because A is an ally of theirs meaning they'll get advantages then of course they'd want A to win. If claimant B winning means they'd need to go to ground because B has only not been openly trying to kill them because of politics then they wouldn't want that person to win. I know those are extreme, but I just mean take why they want a specific claimant to win into account when making decisions. Granted by the same extension I trust you would not give claimant C a chance in hell of winning if you've gathered that your actual players despise C and would be miserable in a scenario where C was given more focus.<br /> <br /> You could add in of course whether or not options are long term exclusive ie if their preferred claimant were to not get the title and down the line the winner died would their claimant be able to then get the title. I will also suggest remembering you have to run it and if you think A winning would be more fun than B that is a good reason to lean that way as opposed to just fate. If you don't care either way then yeah coin toss. This is also part of why I say I go towards chance if I feel both sides are equal if they prefer A, but B has the superior claim and is almost certain to win then the group not getting involved to try and tip the scales is the consequence of their inaction. I don't mean as a punishment just a, &quot;Hey should we maybe do some stuff to bolster A's chances of winning?&quot; &quot;Nah, we have more important things to do.&quot; Could reasonably result in things not going their way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-28T00:59:49-07:00","id":1513051,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nalak42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1512998-how-do-you-handle-disputes?p=1513047#post1513047\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well part of it would also come down to why the players would prefer one side over the other I'd think. If the party prefers claimant A because A is an ally of theirs meaning they'll get advantages then of course they'd want A to win. If claimant B winning means they'd need to go to ground because B has only not been openly trying to kill them because of politics then they wouldn't want that person to win. I know those are extreme, but I just mean take why they want a specific claimant to win into account when making decisions. Granted by the same extension I trust you would not give claimant C a chance in hell of winning if you've gathered that your actual players despise C and would be miserable in a scenario where C was given more focus.<br /> <br /> You could add in of course whether or not options are long term exclusive ie if their preferred claimant were to not get the title and down the line the winner died would their claimant be able to then get the title. I will also suggest remembering you have to run it and if you think A winning would be more fun than B that is a good reason to lean that way as opposed to just fate. If you don't care either way then yeah coin toss. This is also part of why I say I go towards chance if I feel both sides are equal if they prefer A, but B has the superior claim and is almost certain to win then the group not getting involved to try and tip the scales is the consequence of their inaction. I don't mean as a punishment just a, &quot;Hey should we maybe do some stuff to bolster A's chances of winning?&quot; &quot;Nah, we have more important things to do.&quot; Could reasonably result in things not going their way.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see your point. I guess I needed to make things clearer to the specific case I'm talking about. Even though my question was more &quot;generic&quot;, so to speak. I mean, I wanted to know how other Storytellers here handle conflicts that are beyond the control of the PCs to unfold. I know Storytellers who would play or just decide beforehand the results of whatever conflict might happen in their games, as I sated before, during the last 20 years I've been storytelling, I've used many different approaches to these scenarios, but most of the time I like the idea of letting a part of it being &quot;in the hands of fate&quot;, so dice and any other means to simulate that.<br /> <br /> Now talking about the specific scenario, both possibilities are interesting from a narrative perspective, but they both change dramatically the situation of the PCs in my game. Let's say candidate A is the one the group support, they would surely see the benefit of his win as he's an ally to two characters in the group (there's one PC that hates him, but still prefer him to the other and two others that are not close collaborators, but they would be rewarded indirectly if &quot;candidate A&quot; win, the last one is a Malkavian who literally &quot;doesn't care&quot;). A victory for &quot;Candidate B&quot; means at least half of the group of PCs would have to kneel before him and/or make amends or be hunted/destroyed in the short term. The thing is, &quot;Candidate B&quot; is the main antagonist of the game and he has built throughout the game his powerbase and the PCs are quite aware of that, his victory would be bad for half the group/and/or potentially mean potential destruction for them, but it would be interesting for the story. Alas, but the thing is &quot;Candidate A&quot; however beneficial for the group is quite &quot;unrealistic&quot;, so to speak, that means that If I was to give him a chance, I'd have to give it through &quot;luck/fate&quot;, as it would make no sense for him to be the victor just because. I like coming here to ask for opinions regarding these things because after more than 2 decades of storytelling I want to make it more fun for me (I also write, but Storytelling for me is interesting because I have to improvise upon unexpected things, that is a very important aspect of it for me, especially after such a long time in the trade) and giving &quot;fate&quot; a chance is always interesting, so I came here to ask if I should give &quot;candidate A&quot; a chance through dice or something similar, even though all the odds favor &quot;candidate B&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-28T01:03:23-07:00","id":1513052,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well part of it would also come down to why the players would prefer one side over the other I'd think. If the party prefers claimant A because A is an ally of theirs meaning they'll get advantages then of course they'd want A to win. If claimant B winning means they'd need to go to ground because B has only not been openly trying to kill them because of politics then they wouldn't want that person to win. I know those are extreme, but I just mean take why they want a specific claimant to win into account when making decisions. Granted by the same extension I trust you would not give claimant C a chance in hell of winning if you've gathered that your actual players despise C and would be miserable in a scenario where C was given more focus.<br /> <br /> You could add in of course whether or not options are long term exclusive ie if their preferred claimant were to not get the title and down the line the winner died would their claimant be able to then get the title. I will also suggest remembering you have to run it and if you think A winning would be more fun than B that is a good reason to lean that way as opposed to just fate. If you don't care either way then yeah coin toss. This is also part of why I say I go towards chance if I feel both sides are equal if they prefer A, but B has the superior claim and is almost certain to win then the group not getting involved to try and tip the scales is the consequence of their inaction. I don't mean as a punishment just a, &quot;Hey should we maybe do some stuff to bolster A's chances of winning?&quot; &quot;Nah, we have more important things to do.&quot; Could reasonably result in things not going their way.\u200b </div> </div> </div>Unapproved post are driving me nuts, here I go again...<br /> <br /> I see your point. I guess I needed to make things clearer to the specific case I'm talking about. Even though my question was more &quot;generic&quot;, so to speak. I mean, I wanted to know how other Storytellers here handle conflicts that are beyond the control of the PCs to unfold. I know Storytellers who would play or just decide beforehand the results of whatever conflict might happen in their games, as I sated before, during the last 20 years I've been storytelling, I've used many different approaches to these scenarios, but most of the time I like the idea of letting a part of it being &quot;in the hands of fate&quot;, so dice and any other means to simulate that.<br /> <br /> Now talking about the specific scenario, both possibilities are interesting from a narrative perspective, but they both change dramatically the situation of the PCs in my game. Let's say candidate A is the one the group support, they would surely see the benefit of his win as he's an ally to two characters in the group (there's one PC that hates him, but still prefer him to the other and two others that are not close collaborators, but they would be rewarded indirectly if &quot;candidate A&quot; win, the last one is a Malkavian who literally &quot;doesn't care&quot;). A victory for &quot;Candidate B&quot; means at least half of the group of PCs would have to kneel before him and/or make amends or be hunted/destroyed in the short term. The thing is, &quot;Candidate B&quot; is the main antagonist of the game and he has built throughout the game his powerbase and the PCs are quite aware of that, his victory would be bad for half the group/and/or potentially mean potential destruction for them, but it would be interesting for the story. Alas, but the thing is &quot;Candidate A&quot; however beneficial for the group is quite &quot;unrealistic&quot;, so to speak, that means that If I was to give him a chance, I'd have to give it through &quot;luck/fate&quot;, as it would make no sense for him to be the victor just because. I like coming here to ask for opinions regarding these things because after more than 2 decades of storytelling I want to make it more fun for me (I also write, but Storytelling for me is interesting because I have to improvise upon unexpected things, that is a very important aspect of it for me, especially after such a long time in the trade) and giving &quot;fate&quot; a chance is always interesting, so I came here to ask if I should give &quot;candidate A&quot; a chance through dice or something similar, even though all the odds favor &quot;candidate B&quot;.\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-28T21:58:20-07:00","id":1513159,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1512998-how-do-you-handle-disputes?p=1513051#post1513051\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I see your point. I guess I needed to make things clearer to the specific case I'm talking about. Even though my question was more &quot;generic&quot;, so to speak. I mean, I wanted to know how other Storytellers here handle conflicts that are beyond the control of the PCs to unfold. I know Storytellers who would play or just decide beforehand the results of whatever conflict might happen in their games, as I sated before, during the last 20 years I've been storytelling, I've used many different approaches to these scenarios, but most of the time I like the idea of letting a part of it being &quot;in the hands of fate&quot;, so dice and any other means to simulate that.<br /> <br /> Now talking about the specific scenario, both possibilities are interesting from a narrative perspective, but they both change dramatically the situation of the PCs in my game. Let's say candidate A is the one the group support, they would surely see the benefit of his win as he's an ally to two characters in the group (there's one PC that hates him, but still prefer him to the other and two others that are not close collaborators, but they would be rewarded indirectly if &quot;candidate A&quot; win, the last one is a Malkavian who literally &quot;doesn't care&quot;). A victory for &quot;Candidate B&quot; means at least half of the group of PCs would have to kneel before him and/or make amends or be hunted/destroyed in the short term. The thing is, &quot;Candidate B&quot; is the main antagonist of the game and he has built throughout the game his powerbase and the PCs are quite aware of that, his victory would be bad for half the group/and/or potentially mean potential destruction for them, but it would be interesting for the story. Alas, but the thing is &quot;Candidate A&quot; however beneficial for the group is quite &quot;unrealistic&quot;, so to speak, that means that If I was to give him a chance, I'd have to give it through &quot;luck/fate&quot;, as it would make no sense for him to be the victor just because. I like coming here to ask for opinions regarding these things because after more than 2 decades of storytelling I want to make it more fun for me (I also write, but Storytelling for me is interesting because I have to improvise upon unexpected things, that is a very important aspect of it for me, especially after such a long time in the trade) and giving &quot;fate&quot; a chance is always interesting, so I came here to ask if I should give &quot;candidate A&quot; a chance through dice or something similar, even though all the odds favor &quot;candidate B&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>I get that fate and luck are the more interesting, but a thing to consider. How do you see A winning? Like if the proverbial dice roll comes up and says A wins how did they win if the only reason you feel they have a chance is because it is possible for the dice to come up that way? I guess I'm not asking for specifics, just consider that because unless the players don't know the political situation and are unaware that A's are crap then that's probably going to come up. Not to mention you can get more plot hooks and angles based on the how. Maybe A managed to convince a number of swing votes through deals, and turned a few of B's assets which means certain problems are still on the table because they were threats if they were connected to the throne, etc.<br /> <br /> Or heck A is already a friend to at least some of the group. So have A recruit the group to even things up. Oh there's probably not enough time to swing the vote so A will definitely win, but maybe they can swing things so A has a solid chance at winning and then it comes down to the dice roll to see how the last things no one was able to influence play out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-28T22:34:16-07:00","id":1513163,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nalak42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1512998-how-do-you-handle-disputes?p=1513159#post1513159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I get that fate and luck are the more interesting, but a thing to consider. How do you see A winning? Like if the proverbial dice roll comes up and says A wins how did they win if the only reason you feel they have a chance is because it is possible for the dice to come up that way? I guess I'm not asking for specifics, just consider that because unless the players don't know the political situation and are unaware that A's are crap then that's probably going to come up. Not to mention you can get more plot hooks and angles based on the how. Maybe A managed to convince a number of swing votes through deals, and turned a few of B's assets which means certain problems are still on the table because they were threats if they were connected to the throne, etc.<br /> <br /> Or heck A is already a friend to at least some of the group. So have A recruit the group to even things up. Oh there's probably not enough time to swing the vote so A will definitely win, but maybe they can swing things so A has a solid chance at winning and then it comes down to the dice roll to see how the last things no one was able to influence play out.</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing is the group know that B is the favorite, they would be surprised if A won. I know how to properly justify A's victory if he wins through the &quot;hand of fate&quot;, so to speak, in fact he surprisingly won in a best of 5 by 3x0. So then I decided to complicate things further to give him an edge, and that is that he had the unknown support of a third part. I just use the dice when there's a chance, however small it might be, but when I can justify somehow the victory and just don't think it's interesting to decide by myself. I like this element of unpredictability in my games, so that some things are beyond even my control as a Storyteller. His results really surprised me, by the way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-29T16:01:30-07:00","id":1513246,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1512998-how-do-you-handle-disputes#post1512998\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, I bring another interesting topic (well, at least I think it's interresting lol) for discussion, mainly aimed at Storytellers. So, how do you handle disputes/battles/conflicts or whatever that only involves NPCs in your games?<br /> I mean, surely we as Storytellers can simply decide who wins a dispute, but for some reason I don't think it's particularly fun and sometimes when I have a chance I create a dispute (when I was younger, I used to even make all the rolls and describe the scenes even if it happened outside the PCs view, nerdy I know lol) involving dice or something else, but then it made me curious at how other Storytellers handle such things. Do you always predetermine the results of conflicts that only involve NPCs or do you sometimes make a &quot;mini-game&quot;, roll dice or any other method to decide the result?</div> </div> </div> </div>Some VtM books, guides, and supplements have maps, schemes, pictures, schemes, and drawings that indicate strages, strenght qualities, and types of relations and interactions between groups and characters. Some of these could definitely help in regard to this. Also - general and normal tendencies, ideas, design, and priorities derived from these maps and drawings could be useful and worthwhile. Some of the comments and descriptions are certainly very appropriate and unique.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-03T15:30:55-07:00","id":1513821,"title":"","content":"I tend to agree with HeavyArms, a few times I've found myself trying to properly assign statistics and odds to certain offscreen scenarios and situations, and then I realize, it's storytelling, I'm the storyteller, I decide which way this goes based on what will either be the most entertaining or engaging for my players. <br /> <br /> That being said, for well telegraphed things I might make some simplified sheets and motivation notes for Key NPCs and have players take over a sort of 'cutaway' or 'meanwhile' scene to decide what the large background characters do. Or I may call for a luck roll from the players so that they can get involved in the outcome at least.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27296-devidramoth","name":"Devidramoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27296_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-03T15:49:29-07:00","id":1513822,"title":"","content":"I try to only roll dice when the outcome is undecided or unknown to me. So if I want a specific result to happen as ST, it just happens. But if a situation develops where I do not know who should win or what should happen (either because I am unsure what to do or just don't care what happens as ST), then I let the dice decide. I may use a single die roll at even odds or give weight to one side more than the other, or I may decide to base it on some attribute or power (someone's Mental + Knowledge roll versus the others for example) if I think that will produce better results. But I try to keep it to one dice roll to expedite the game.<br /> <br /> I typically only do this when the PCs are not involved. But sometimes because of their actions, they may cause a reaction. Or if something has been building behind the scenes, and it is time for that conflict to happen or be resolved, then I might do that. I tend to prefer a sandbox style of play (while still running personal plots and plan for something each game session as well as a &quot;chronicle mystery&quot;), so something these things happen because NPCs have autonomy too. But I also try to limit NPCs being too active on their plots separate from the PCs because it can get too confusing to the players.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}